Straight answer: no, and not for a long time. Worth being clear about this now rather than you wondering in three months why nothing's happening.

Why not those searches

"PM Surya Ghar" — that result page is owned by the government. pmsuryaghar.gov.in, the Ministry of New and Renewable Energy, PIB press releases, then major news and SBI. Google strongly favours official sources for a government scheme by name. A three-hour-old site on a free subdomain is not going to sit among them.

"solar installation India / West Bengal" — different problem. That's a high-value commercial search, and it's fought over by installers with real ad budgets, plus JustDial and IndiaMART. Those companies spend more per month on SEO than this whole project costs.

Both are what people call head terms: short, huge volume, and won by whoever has the most authority. You have none yet — authority mostly comes from other sites linking to you, and nothing links to you.

What you can realistically win

Not the broad search. The specific one. Someone typing:

▎ "pm surya ghar subsidy for 2.5 kw"
▎ "solar loan emi 3kw sbi west bengal"
▎ "pnb solar loan interest rate above 2 lakh"

Far fewer people search those — but the ones who do are exactly your visitors, and the competition is thin because most sites can't answer them. They copy the brochure line "₹78,000 for 3 KW and above" and stop.

You publish that a 2.32 KW system gets ₹65,760 — pro-rata, exact to the rupee, checked against real portal payments. Nobody else has that. That's the kind of page Google eventually rewards, because it's the only one that actually answers the question.

That's why the explainer and FAQ mattered more than any of today's setup work.

Realistic timeline

- Now: indexed. Findable if someone searches your exact site or a very unusual phrase
- 1–3 months: starts appearing for those long, specific queries. A trickle
- 6+ months: meaningful traffic — if content keeps growing and some real links appear

Anyone promising faster is selling something.

The two things that would actually move it

1. Buy solaremicalculator.in (~₹700–1,000/year). A .pages.dev address is a free shared subdomain — it works fine, but it reads as temporary to both users and Google, and AdSense reviewers take a real domain more seriously. This is the single highest-value ₹1,000 you could spend here.
2. Get a few genuine links. Answer PM Surya Ghar questions in West Bengal Facebook groups or solar forums, and link your calculator where it genuinely helps. Ten real links from relevant places beat a thousand junk ones.

And keep writing the specific stuff you know and others don't. That's your actual advantage — not the code, which anyone could rebuild in a weekend.

✻ Cooked for 32s

❯ but my site menation the keyword pm surya so wont it show up in search results along with pm surya

Ah — this is the thing to understand about SEO, and it trips up nearly everyone. Let me be clear about it.

Mentioning a keyword gets you into the results. It does not decide where in the results.

Search "PM Surya Ghar" and Google will say something like "About 12,00,000 results". Your page is genuinely one of them — you're in there. The problem is you might be number 400.

Nobody sees number 400. Most people never leave the first ten. Being on result page 40 and not existing at all are the same thing in practice.

The bit that's easy to miss

Google isn't matching — it's ranking. Every one of those 12 lakh pages mentions PM Surya Ghar. Google's whole job is deciding which ten go on top. That ordering is driven by:

- how many other sites link to you (you have zero)
- how long the site has existed and been trusted (yours: three hours)
- whether people who click stay and read, or bounce straight back
- how well the page matches the specific thing someone asked

Mentioning the phrase is the entry requirement. Everyone clears it. It's not a tiebreaker.

And to head off the obvious next thought: repeating the keyword more times makes it worse, not better. That worked in about 1998. Google now calls it keyword stuffing and demotes pages for it.

Prove it to yourself in 30 seconds

Take an unusual exact sentence from your own page — say a chunk of your subsidy explanation — put it in quote marks, and search Google for it:

"Notice that everything from 3 KW upward receives the same"

You'll likely be number 1, possibly the only result. Same site, same day, same authority.

Now search PM Surya Ghar — you're nowhere.

Nothing about your site changed between those two searches. What changed is how many other pages were competing. For the exact sentence: none. For the scheme name: over a million, including the government's own.

That's the whole game. You don't win by mentioning the popular words — you win by being the best answer to a question few others can answer.

Which, with your subsidy figures and real bank rates, is a game you can actually win. Just not that particular search.
